{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbhYAqrzKQwaEWjW36Kc", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Yes, but only with `--dry-run`, and check where `EXPORT_BUCKET` points first.\n\nA team note in the project's memory store (EngramDB) warns that in staging, `EXPORT_BUCKET` still points at the **production** bucket, left over from the 2025 migration. Running the export against staging without `--dry-run` could overwrite the real prod invoice reports.\n\nThe script picks its destination from that variable alone (`scripts/export.py:10`). The `--env staging` flag only labels the run and doesn't change the bucket. I couldn't confirm the staging value from the repo because it's set in the environment, not the code.\n\nTo test it safely:\n\n```bash\n# 1. See where it would write\necho \"$EXPORT_BUCKET\"\n\n# 2. Run in dry-run mode\npython scripts/export.py --env staging --dry-run\n```\n\nThe script prints `exporting staging to <bucket> (dry_run=True)`, so you can see the target. If it names a prod bucket, the note is still accurate.\n\nTo do a real (non-dry-run) staging test, override the bucket for that one command with a staging or scratch bucket:\n\n```bash\nEXPORT_BUCKET=<your-staging-bucket> python scripts/export.py --env staging\n```\n\nThe longer-term fix is to point staging's `EXPORT_BUCKET` at a staging bucket, or to make the script refuse a prod bucket unless `--env prod` is set. I can add that guard if you'd like."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 766, "cache_read_input_tokens": 24794, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 766}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f0